/*! \page Materials_newHost Adding a host material

Adding a host material is a declaration, not a change of the solver. The \ref Core_intro never derives a rate: a host registers its size-resolved rate kernels as precomputed arrays.

A new material needs a folder materials/\<name\> on both sides of the code:

\verbatim
radcluster_code/py_utils/materials/<name>/
radcluster_code/cpp_utils/materials/<name>/
\endverbatim

On the Python side the material:
1. declares its populations (see \ref Core_rag)
2. adds its Edge families, each of one of the ten \ref Core_edgeClasses
3. registers a rate kernel for every edge
4. builds the \ref Core_stateLayout that packs the populations into the ODE state vector

On the C++ side the material supplies the right-hand-side callbacks. The solver driver reaches them only through the function pointer UserData::rhs_fn, so that the files in cpp_utils/core have no compile-time dependency on the kernel arithmetic of a host.

\section Materials_newHost_order Order of work

New physics follows the same order for every material:
1. develop it in the Python graph walker (see \ref Core_graphWalker)
2. pass the conservation gate (see \ref Verification_intro)
3. mirror it in the C++ kernels
4. calibrate

The EUROFER-97 declaration is the worked example. See \ref Materials_eurofer97.

*/
